{% set local_problem %}
<p>Albertville and Sand Mountain have plenty of trees — pines, oaks, and everything in between. Those trees dump debris into your gutters year-round: pine needles in spring and summer, leaves in fall, twigs and seeds all the time.</p>

<p>Without guards, you're looking at cleaning gutters 2-4 times a year. Either you're climbing ladders yourself (which gets less appealing every year) or you're paying someone $150-200 per visit. That adds up fast.</p>

<p>Gutter guards break that cycle. They keep the debris out while letting water flow through. After a few years, they've paid for themselves — and you haven't climbed a single ladder.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Gutter guards are covers or screens that fit over or inside your gutters. They let water in while blocking leaves, pine needles, and other debris.</p>

<p>Different types work better for different situations. Micro-mesh guards block even fine debris like pine needles. Screen guards handle larger leaves. Solid covers use surface tension to pull water in while debris slides off.</p>

<p>We'll look at what trees are around your home and recommend the guard type that makes the most sense. No point in selling you the most expensive option if a simpler guard will do the job.</p>
{% endset %}

